  "record_bodies_before": {
    "courier-relay/protocol-1-delivery-identity": "Courier Relay protocol 1 delivery identity and dedup rule. Applies to every protocol-1 request (count and any later protocol-1 command), and stays in force for protocol \"1\" after a newer protocol becomes current, because explicitly named supported protocols keep their own behavior.\n\nSource: bundled provider contract vendor/protocol-1.md; applied in trial-rme (count command).\n\nRule:\n- Identity: delivery_id ALONE identifies a delivery, across all accounts. account_id is NOT part of the key. The same delivery_id under a different account_id is another receipt of the SAME delivery, so it is a duplicate, not a separate acceptance. Rationale from the contract: the identity comes from the provider's delivery-ID allocation guarantee, and account attribution alone does not change that guarantee. This matters because trial-rme's own requests carry at most one account, so the account-independence is not observable in that issue's data and must not be re-derived from it.\n- Winner: exactly one receipt is accepted per identity — earliest occurred_at compared as a UTC instant (offsets may move a receipt across a local date boundary; compare instants, never date/local text). Ties on the same instant are broken by the smallest record_id in Unicode code-point order.\n- All other receipts for that identity are duplicates.\n- Accepted receipts retain their original fields, original occurred_at spelling, and payload, and are presented in original input order (relevant to later commands that emit receipts, not just counts).\n\nProtocol selection: an omitted \"protocol\" means the current protocol; protocol \"1\" was current at the time of trial-rme. Response objects carry exactly their named fields. As of trial-ym7 the current protocol is \"2\" (key courier-relay/protocol-2-delivery-identity), so an omitted protocol no longer selects this rule and reaching it requires explicit \"protocol\":\"1\". Protocol 1 itself is unrevised: this record remains the whole agreement for explicit \"1\", including its earliest-wins winner and account-independent identity, both of which protocol 2 reverses.",
    "courier-relay/protocol-2-delivery-identity": "Courier Relay protocol 2 delivery identity and dedup rule, and protocol 2 as the current protocol. Applies to every protocol-2 request (count, summary, and any later protocol-2 command) and to any request that omits \"protocol\", because an omitted protocol selects the current protocol.\n\nSource: the provider permanently adopted protocol 2 and the integration owner approved using it as the current protocol; complete contract quoted in trial-ym7 (adopt the provider's protocol 2 contract), applied there to count and summary. Protocol 1 is NOT retired: it remains supported and unchanged under explicit \"protocol\":\"1\" — see key courier-relay/protocol-1-delivery-identity.\n\nRule (protocol 2):\n- Identity: the PAIR (account_id, delivery_id) identifies a delivery. account_id IS part of the key, so the same delivery_id under a different account_id is a SEPARATE delivery and is accepted, not a duplicate. This is the deliberate reversal of protocol 1, where delivery_id alone was the key across all accounts. Rationale carried over from the contract: the identity comes from the provider's delivery-ID allocation guarantee, and account attribution alone does not change that guarantee.\n- Winner: exactly one receipt is accepted per delivery identity — the LATEST occurred_at compared as a UTC instant (protocol 1 accepts the earliest; this is the second deliberate reversal). Offsets may move a receipt across a local date boundary, so compare instants, never date/local text. Ties on the same instant are broken by the smallest record_id in Unicode code-point order (same tiebreak as protocol 1).\n- All other receipts for that identity are duplicates.\n- Accepted receipts retain their original fields, original occurred_at spelling, and payload, and are presented in original input order (relevant to later commands that emit receipts, not just counts).\n- Scope: the agreement applies to the ENTIRE supplied receipt set regardless of receipt dates — there is no cutover date and no grandfathering of older receipts under protocol 1. Empty sets give zero accepted and zero duplicates; a single receipt gives one accepted and zero duplicates.\n\nProtocol selection: omitted \"protocol\" selects the current protocol, which is now \"2\"; explicit \"2\" selects the same agreement; explicit \"1\" selects the original provider contract. An explicitly named supported protocol keeps its own behavior after a newer version becomes current, so this rule for \"2\" survives a future current-version change. Responses report the protocol actually used and carry exactly their named fields."
  },
